   223	// JSONMetadataPath returns a MySQL/Dolt JSON path expression for the given
   224	// metadata key. The key is always quoted so that "gc.routed_to" produces
   225	// '$."gc.routed_to"' instead of '$.gc.routed_to' (which dolt interprets as a
   226	// nested path: {gc: {routed_to: ...}}); quoting is valid for plain keys too,
   227	// so no character list needs to stay in sync with validMetadataKeyRe. Slash
   228	// and mixed-case keys are proven to round-trip through the real Dolt/
   229	// go-mysql-server JSON path parser (see TestMetadataFilterSuite's
   230	// MetadataFieldMatchSlashKey and MetadataFieldMatchMixedCaseKey subtests in
   231	// cmd/bd/metadata_filter_test.go).
   232	//
   233	// Backslashes and quotes are also escaped, but every production caller
   234	// (AppendMetadataClauses, ApplyMetadataEdits) validates the key with
   235	// ValidateMetadataKey first, which rejects `"` and `\`, so those escaping
   236	// branches are unreachable in practice today and are exercised only by the
   237	// string-level unit test in metadata_jsonpath_test.go, not against the real
   238	// SQL engines. Treat that escaping as defense-in-depth, not a proven
   239	// contract, unless a caller starts passing unvalidated keys here.
   240	func JSONMetadataPath(key string) string {
   241		return `$."` + jsonPathEscaper.Replace(key) + `"`
